<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-application-of-derivatives-to-kinematics" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Application of Derivatives to Kinematics</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.0 Calculus
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.2 Differentiation I
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p>
                        Apply derivatives to kinematics in different situations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    Discover that differentiating a position function gives the velocity function,
                    by comparing the AROC estimates from the average rates of change section
                    with exact instantaneous values from the derivative.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Remind learners of the stone problem from <xref ref="sec-average-rates-of-change" text="title"/>.
                    Ask: <em>"We estimated the velocity over one-second intervals — can we now find
                    the exact velocity at any single instant?"</em>
                    Circulate during parts (a) and (b); encourage learners to notice that
                    <m>s'(t)</m> lands between the two AROC values on either side of <m>t</m>.
                    Part (e) is a first encounter with the second derivative — frame it as
                    <em>"differentiating the velocity"</em> before introducing the notation.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="activity-stone-revisited">
        <title>The Same Stone, Revisited</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                In <xref ref="sec-average-rates-of-change" text="title"/> we studied a stone thrown
                vertically upward with height <m>s(t) = 20t - 5t^2</m> metres after <m>t</m> seconds.
                We computed the average rate of change over each one-second interval:
            </p>
            <table>
                <title>Average velocity (m/s) over one-second intervals</title>
                <tabular halign="center">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                        <cell>Interval</cell>
                        <cell><m>[0,1]</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>[1,2]</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>[2,3]</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>[3,4]</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>AROC (m/s)</cell>
                        <cell>15</cell>
                        <cell>5</cell>
                        <cell><m>-5</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>-15</m></cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <p>
                Now we have the power rule. Use it to answer the following.
            </p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Find <m>s'(t)</m>. What are the units of <m>s'(t)</m>?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Compute <m>s'(0)</m>, <m>s'(1)</m>, <m>s'(2)</m>, <m>s'(3)</m>, <m>s'(4)</m>.
                        Record your values alongside the AROC table above.
                        What do you notice about each value of <m>s'(t)</m> compared to the
                        AROC values on either side of <m>t</m>?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        At what time does <m>s'(t) = 0</m>? What is the stone doing at that moment?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        What does it mean that <m>s'(3) = -10</m>? Is the stone still in the air at <m>t = 3</m>?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Differentiate <m>s'(t)</m> once more to get a new function <m>a(t) = (s')'(t)</m>.
                        What value does <m>a(t)</m> take? What does this tell you about how quickly
                        the stone is gaining or losing speed?
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                The interactive graph below plots <m>s(t)</m> and its derivative <m>s'(t)</m>
                against the same time axis. Drag the time marker <m>t_0</m> along the <m>t</m>-axis:
                the orange tangent to <m>s(t)</m> has slope equal to the stone's velocity, which is
                exactly the height of the orange point on the <m>s'(t)</m> graph at that instant.
                The green arrow shows the stone's direction of motion — watch it shrink to nothing
                at <m>t = 2</m> (the top of the flight) and then reverse as the stone falls.
            </p>
            <interactive xml:id="interactive-kinematics-velocity" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="1:1" source="jsxgraph/kinematics-velocity.js">
                <slate xml:id="kinematics-velocity" surface="jsxboard" aspect="1:1"/>
                <instructions>
                    <p>Drag <m>t_0</m> along the <m>t</m>-axis. Blue is height <m>s(t)</m>; orange is velocity <m>s'(t)</m>. At the top of the flight the velocity is zero; after that it is negative — the stone is falling.</p>
                </instructions>
            </interactive>
        </statement>
        <solution component="lesson-plan">
            <p>
                (a) <m>s'(t) = 20 - 10t</m> m/s.
            </p>
            <p>
                (b) <m>s'(0)=20,\ s'(1)=10,\ s'(2)=0,\ s'(3)=-10,\ s'(4)=-20</m>.
                Each value lies exactly halfway between the AROC values on either side
                (e.g. <m>s'(1) = 10</m> lies between AROC values 15 and 5). The instantaneous
                velocity "splits" the two average velocities — a geometric consequence of the
                parabola's symmetry, and a preview of why the derivative is the limit of AROC.
            </p>
            <p>
                (c) <m>s'(t)=0</m> when <m>20-10t=0</m>, i.e. <m>t=2</m>. The stone is at its
                highest point — momentarily neither rising nor falling.
            </p>
            <p>
                (d) <m>s'(3) = -10</m> m/s means the stone is moving downward at 10 m/s.
                Check: <m>s(3) = 60 - 45 = 15 \gt 0</m>, so yes — still in the air.
            </p>
            <p>
                (e) <m>a(t) = (s')' = (20-10t)' = -10</m> m/s². The stone loses 10 m/s of
                upward velocity every second — constant because gravity is constant.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    5 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    Formalise the chain position <m>\to</m> velocity <m>\to</m> acceleration
                    as successive derivatives, and establish the sign conventions for direction.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <insight>
        <title>Key Ideas</title>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Differentiating a position function gives the <em>velocity</em> function:
                        <m>v(t) = s'(t)</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Differentiating velocity gives <em>acceleration</em>:
                        <m>a(t) = v'(t) = s''(t)</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        If <m>v(t) \gt 0</m> the object moves in the positive direction;
                        if <m>v(t) \lt 0</m> it moves in the negative direction.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>v(t) = 0</m> means the object is momentarily stationary — this
                        is often the moment of maximum or minimum displacement.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </insight>

    <insight xml:id="PRECALC-i4LvslBUwY" original="PRECALC/modules/m49455/index.cnxml" license="CC-BY-4.0">
        <title>Instantaneous Velocity</title>
        <p>
            Let <m>s(t)</m> be the position of an object at time <m>t</m>.
            The <term>instantaneous velocity</term> at time <m>t</m> is
            <md>v(t) = s'(t).</md>
            Velocity is positive when the object moves in the chosen positive direction and
            negative when it moves in the opposite direction.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <insight xml:id="insight-acceleration">
        <title>Acceleration</title>
        <p>
            The <term>acceleration</term> of the object at time <m>t</m> is
            <md>a(t) = v'(t) = s''(t).</md>
            The notation <m>s''(t)</m> means <q>differentiate <m>s</m> twice</q>.
            Acceleration has units of m/s² (metres per second per second).
            Constant acceleration means the velocity changes at a steady rate.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <p>
        To find the maximum (or minimum) height of a projectile, set <m>v(t) = 0</m> and
        solve for <m>t</m>, then substitute back into <m>s(t)</m>.
        A negative velocity simply means the object is moving in the direction of
        decreasing <m>s</m> — for a ball thrown upward, this means it is falling.
        Throughout this section we use <m>g = 10\text{ m/s}^2</m>, so free-fall
        contributes a term <m>-5t^2</m> to the position function.
    </p>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-stone-velocity">
        <title>Velocity and Maximum Height</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A stone is thrown vertically upward at <m>20</m> m/s from ground level.
                Its height after <m>t</m> seconds is <m>s(t) = 20t - 5t^2</m>.
            </p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li><p>Find <m>v(t)</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Verify that the initial velocity matches the problem.</p></li>
                <li><p>Find the time at which the stone reaches its maximum height.</p></li>
                <li><p>Find the maximum height.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                (a) By the power rule, <m>v(t) = s'(t) = 20 - 10t</m> m/s.
            </p>
            <p>
                (b) <m>v(0) = 20 - 10(0) = 20</m> m/s. This matches the given initial velocity. <m>\checkmark</m>
            </p>
            <p>
                (c) The stone is at its highest point when <m>v(t) = 0</m>:
                <md>20 - 10t = 0 \implies t = 2 \text{ seconds.}</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                (d) <m>s(2) = 20(2) - 5(2)^2 = 40 - 20 = 20</m> m.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-particle-motion">
        <title>Velocity, Acceleration, and Direction</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A particle moves along a straight line. Its position (in metres) at time
                <m>t</m> seconds is <m>s(t) = t^3 - 6t^2 + 9t</m>.
            </p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li><p>Find <m>v(t)</m> and <m>a(t)</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>When is the particle stationary?</p></li>
                <li><p>When is the particle moving in the negative direction?</p></li>
                <li><p>Find the acceleration at <m>t = 4</m>.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                (a)
                <md>
                    <mrow>v(t) \amp= s'(t) = 3t^2 - 12t + 9 = 3(t-1)(t-3)</mrow>
                    <mrow>a(t) \amp= v'(t) = 6t - 12</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                (b) The particle is stationary when <m>v(t) = 0</m>:
                <md>3(t-1)(t-3) = 0 \implies t = 1 \text{ s or } t = 3 \text{ s.}</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                (c) <m>v(t) \lt 0</m> when <m>3(t-1)(t-3) \lt 0</m>, i.e. when <m>1 \lt t \lt 3</m>.
                Between <m>t=1</m> and <m>t=3</m> the particle moves in the negative direction.
            </p>
            <p>
                (d) <m>a(4) = 6(4) - 12 = 12 \text{ m/s}^2</m>. The particle is accelerating in the
                positive direction at <m>t = 4</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-matatu-kinematics">
        <title>A Matatu on the Nairobi Road</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A matatu travels along a straight road from Nairobi. Its displacement (in km)
                from the city centre after <m>t</m> minutes is
                <m>s(t) = 2t^2 - t</m> for <m>0 \leq t \leq 10</m>.
            </p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li><p>Find the matatu's velocity at <m>t = 3</m> minutes.</p></li>
                <li><p>Find the time at which the velocity equals <m>15</m> km/min.</p></li>
                <li><p>Find the acceleration. Is it constant?</p></li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                (a) <m>v(t) = s'(t) = 4t - 1</m>. At <m>t = 3</m>:
                <m>v(3) = 4(3) - 1 = 11</m> km/min.
            </p>
            <p>
                (b) Set <m>v(t) = 15</m>:
                <md>4t - 1 = 15 \implies 4t = 16 \implies t = 4 \text{ minutes.}</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                (c) <m>a(t) = v'(t) = 4</m> km/min². Yes — the acceleration is constant,
                meaning the matatu gains speed at a steady rate.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise-ball-ramp">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A ball rolls along a ramp. Its displacement from the top of the ramp after
                    <m>t</m> seconds is <m>s(t) = t^2 - 4t + 3</m> metres.
                </p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Find <m>v(t)</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>When is the ball stationary?</p></li>
                    <li><p>What is the ball's position at that moment?</p></li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    (a) <m>v(t) = 2t - 4</m> m/s.
                </p>
                <p>
                    (b) <m>2t - 4 = 0 \implies t = 2</m> s.
                </p>
                <p>
                    (c) <m>s(2) = 4 - 8 + 3 = -1</m> m. (The ball has moved 1 m past the reference point.)
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise-cyclist-market">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A cyclist's displacement from a market is <m>s(t) = 3t^2 + 2t</m> metres
                    after <m>t</m> seconds.
                    Find the cyclist's velocity and acceleration at <m>t = 5</m> s.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>v(t) = 6t + 2</m>, so <m>v(5) = 32</m> m/s.
                    <m>a(t) = 6</m> m/s² (constant).
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise-cliff-drop">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A stone is dropped from the top of a cliff 80 m above the ground.
                    Taking downward as positive, its displacement is <m>s(t) = 5t^2</m> metres.
                </p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Find the velocity at <m>t = 3</m> s.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find the time at which the stone hits the ground, and the velocity at that moment.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    (a) <m>v(t) = 10t</m>, so <m>v(3) = 30</m> m/s.
                </p>
                <p>
                    (b) <m>s(t) = 80 \implies 5t^2 = 80 \implies t^2 = 16 \implies t = 4</m> s.
                    Velocity on impact: <m>v(4) = 40</m> m/s.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="PRECALC-LAPPE9aTlC" original="PRECALC/modules/m49455/index.cnxml" license="CC-BY-4.0">
            <title>Interpreting Position and Velocity</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A runner runs along a straight east-west road. The function <m>f(t)</m> gives
                    how many metres east of her starting point she is after <m>t</m> seconds.
                    Interpret each of the following.
                </p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><m>f(0) = 0</m></li>
                    <li><m>f(10) = 150</m></li>
                    <li><m>f'(10) = 15</m></li>
                    <li><m>f'(20) = -10</m></li>
                    <li><m>f(40) = -100</m></li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>She starts at her starting point.</li>
                    <li>After 10 s she is 150 m east of her start.</li>
                    <li>At <m>t = 10</m> s she is moving east at 15 m/s.</li>
                    <li>At <m>t = 20</m> s she is moving west at 10 m/s.</li>
                    <li>After 40 s she is 100 m west of her starting point.</li>
                </ol>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise-particle-cubic">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A particle's position is <m>s(t) = 2t^3 - 9t^2 + 12t - 4</m> metres.
                </p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Find when the particle is stationary.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find the acceleration at each stationary point.</p></li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            What does the sign of the acceleration at each stationary point
                            tell you about the motion there?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    (a) <m>v(t) = 6t^2 - 18t + 12 = 6(t-1)(t-2)</m>.
                    Stationary at <m>t = 1</m> s and <m>t = 2</m> s.
                </p>
                <p>
                    (b) <m>a(t) = 12t - 18</m>.
                    <m>a(1) = -6</m> m/s²; <m>a(2) = 6</m> m/s².
                </p>
                <p>
                    (c) At <m>t = 1</m>, the acceleration is negative — the particle has
                    just reached a local maximum in displacement and is about to move in the
                    negative direction. At <m>t = 2</m>, the acceleration is positive — the
                    particle has reached a local minimum and is about to move in the positive
                    direction again.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise-kinematics-simultaneous">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A particle moves so that its position is <m>s(t) = at^2 + bt</m> metres
                    for constants <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.
                    At <m>t = 1</m> s the velocity is <m>7</m> m/s, and at <m>t = 3</m> s the
                    velocity is <m>15</m> m/s.
                </p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Write down two equations in <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Solve for <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find the acceleration of the particle.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    (a) <m>v(t) = 2at + b</m>. The conditions give:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>2a + b \amp= 7</mrow>
                        <mrow>6a + b \amp= 15</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    (b) Subtracting: <m>4a = 8 \implies a = 2</m>. Then <m>b = 7 - 4 = 3</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    (c) <m>a(t) = v'(t) = 2a = 4</m> m/s² (constant).
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

    </exercises>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />

